---
title: Device Choice and Care
---

Choose equipment from configurations that have already completed a field season, then plan care around the conditions the records must survive.

## Learn from field-tested configurations

The table below records deployments team members have actually completed with NAHPU. It is evidence of what a configuration sustained, not an equipment list to copy. Compare the days, specimen counts, and site conditions with your own season before drawing a conclusion from it.

| Field location | Site type | Days | Specimens | Type | Model | Operating system | Accesories |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Java, Indonesia | Tropical mountain | 33| 196 | Tablet | Lenovo Tab P11 Pro | Android  | Logitech K380 keyboard, ProCase for Lenovo Tab P11 Pro 11.2", 2 x 25k mAH power bank |
| Sumatra, Indonesia | Tropical mountain | 115 | 644 | Tablet | iPad Mini 6 | iPadOS | Logitech K380 keyboard, Zugu Case for iPad Mini 6, 2 x 25k mAH power bank |
| Sumatra, Indonesia | Tropical mountain | 115 | >100 | Tablet | iPad Mini 6 | iPadOS | Otterbox iPad Mini 6 case |

Other devices using NAHPU in the field:

- iPhone 13 mini used in Mexico field expeditions

{% aside type="note" title="Product names and endorsements" %}

This page mentions product names and models as examples from fieldwork
experience. These mentions are not endorsements or recommendations by NAHPU.
Evaluate each product for your own needs, budget, and field conditions.

{% /aside %}

Both 115-day deployments used a small tablet with a protective case, a wireless keyboard, and two power banks. A small tablet holds a smaller battery than a full-size one while returning comparable runtime, so a given power bank yields more recharges. A keyboard speeds entry and keeps dirty hands off the screen, which matters when a session runs to hundreds of specimens. NAHPU's interface adapts to a phone screen, so a smartphone you already own is a workable starting point rather than a compromise that blocks the workflow.

## Size power and storage from measured numbers

Measure your own workload rather than adopting a number from another team, then check it against these figures. Four months of fieldwork produced a NAHPU database holding 230 high-resolution images in approximately 820 MB. A 16 GB flash drive therefore carries many full backups of a season at that scale, and a daily project export is far smaller than a full database backup, so a season of daily copies fits comfortably alongside them.

Battery life follows the same rule. An iPad Mini 6 used strictly for data collection, with reduced screen brightness and limited camera use, lasted at least five days on one charge. Photography, location capture, and exports all shorten that, so run a representative NAHPU session that includes them before setting a charging schedule.

Estimate recharges from capacity rather than from the number printed on the power bank. Power banks lose energy as heat during transfer; 60% is a commonly quoted industry-standard efficiency, and manufacturers publish their own figures.

```text
Number of charges = (Power Bank Capacity (mAh) × Efficiency) ÷ Device Battery Capacity (mAh)
```

A 25,000 mAh power bank at 60% efficiency charging an iPad Mini 6, whose battery is 5,124 mAh, gives (25,000 × 0.6) ÷ 5,124 = 2.9 charges. Paired with the five-day runtime above, two such power banks cover roughly a month between mains power — which is why both long deployments in the table carried two.

Recharging the power bank itself is the constraint when a guide can carry it to the nearest village and back. A 25,000 mAh unit with USB-C reaches full in about 4 hours, while a 20,000 mAh unit with micro-USB can take 8 hours or more. Carry a multi-output charger of at least 65 W, ideally one with switchable plugs for the outlets you will meet. See [RavPower's explanation](https://blog.ravpower.com/2023/09/understanding-power-bank-specifications-capacity-mah-charging-efficiency-watt-hours-and-more/) of capacity, efficiency, and watt-hours for the underlying arithmetic.

## Protect batteries and equipment from heat and contamination

Keep devices and power banks out of sustained heat. Leng, Tan & Pecht (2015) cycled lithium-ion cells at 25, 35, 45, and 55 °C and found the aging rate rising with temperature across that range, so a power bank strapped to the outside of a pack in full sun is losing capacity you will want in week twelve. Carry batteries inside the pack, charge in shade, and let a hot device cool before charging it.

Use protection matched to the weather, handling, and transport you expect; every long deployment in the table above ran with a case. Inspect charging ports and cables before use, and protect backup media separately — a case around the tablet does nothing for the flash drive holding the only copy of an export.

Clean the device on a schedule, and treat it as field equipment for hygiene purposes. The recording device travels in the same pack as gear that contacted animals and is handled between sites with the same hands, so it falls under the between-site hygiene measures Phillott et al. (2010) recommend for accessory equipment in their review of field protocols. Fold the device into whatever disinfection routine your project already runs between populations, and keep that routine inside the manufacturer's stated cleaning limits: disconnect cables first, keep liquid out of openings, and use only permitted products and methods. Follow your institution's handling protocol for shared equipment as well.

## Test offline operation

Load required taxa, presets, fonts, and reference files before departure. Rehearse without internet and check which maps or external resources are actually available. A visible map during preparation does not establish that all needed areas will be available offline.

Grant the required permissions and review location readings at the collecting position. Restore connectivity when needed for manual transfers or backup uploads; NAHPU does not automatically synchronize project edits between devices. Confirm the receiving copy before considering a transfer complete.

## References

Leng F, Tan CM & Pecht M (2015). [Effect of temperature on the aging rate of Li ion battery operating above room temperature](https://doi.org/10.1038/srep12967). *Scientific Reports* 5:12967.

Phillott AD, Speare R, Hines HB, Skerratt LF, Meyer E, McDonald KR, Cashins SD, Mendez D & Berger L (2010). [Minimising exposure of amphibians to pathogens during field studies](https://doi.org/10.3354/dao02162). *Diseases of Aquatic Organisms* 92:175–185.

Follow the current device-specific guidance: [Apple cleaning instructions](https://support.apple.com/en-us/103258) and [Samsung cleaning instructions](https://www.samsung.com/us/support/answer/ANS10003416/). For preparation and recovery, see [App Setup](/en/best-practices/app-setup/); for choosing a device against the workflow you will run, see [Device Requirements](/en/usages/devices/).
